     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem, and I can't verify the clinical rationale from here. 5,100 mg/day is twice the labeled maximum for metformin. I don't know of any indication or stage of treatment where that dose is appropriate. The main risk is serious lactic acidosis, and the standard approach to uncontrolled glucose is adding or switching agents, not going above the metformin ceiling.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Enter a dose within range.** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day) right away. I can also enter any other dose up to the maximum.\n- **Use the pharmacy and prescriber override pathway.** If there's a documented reason for exceeding the maximum, such as a protocol, a specialist recommendation, or a trial, it should go through pharmacist review and your hospital's high-dose override process. Then it can be entered directly with that approval on record.\n- **Review the intended regimen.** If the goal is more glycemic control, endocrinology or pharmacy can suggest adding another agent.\n\nIf you'd like one of the in-range options, tell me which and I'll enter it now."
      }
     ],
